<article class="env-proposition" id="06ZB">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06ZB">Proposition <span data-tag="06ZB">9.6.3.10</span>.</a> Let $\lambda $ be a regular cardinal, let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a $\lambda $-cocomplete $\infty $-category and let $W = \{  w_ s: C_ s \rightarrow D_ s \} _{s \in S}$ be a $\lambda $-small collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Assume that: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists a regular cardinal $\kappa &lt; \lambda $ such that each of the objects $C_ s$ and $D_{s}$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every object $E \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and every $s \in S$, the morphism spaces $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( C_ s, E)$ and $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(D_ s, E)$ are essentially $\lambda $-small. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the full subcategory spanned by the $W$-local objects, and let $\overline{W}$ be the smallest collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ containing $W$ which is closed under $\lambda $-small colimits, contains all identity morphisms, and has the two-out-of-three property. Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ is a reflective subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, and $\overline{W}$ is the collection of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$-local equivalences in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Note that the collection of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$-local equivalences in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is closed under $\lambda $-small colimits, contains all identity morphisms, and has the two-out-of-three property. Since every morphism belonging to $W$ is a $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$-local equivalence (by the definition of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$), it follows that every morphism belonging to $\overline{W}$ is a $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$-local equivalence. By virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="05Z2" href="/tag/05Z2">6.2.3.14</a>, it will suffice to show that for every object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, there exists a morphism $f: X \rightarrow Y$ which belongs to $\overline{W}$ where $Y$ is $W$-local. 
    <p>
      For each morphism $w: C \rightarrow D$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, choose a relative codiagonal $\gamma _{w}: D \coprod _{C} D \rightarrow D$ (Variant <a data-tag="04K7" href="/tag/04K7">7.6.2.23</a>). Let $W'$ be the smallest collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which contains $W$ and is closed under the operation $w \mapsto \gamma _{w}$. Note that $\gamma _{w}$ can be regarded as a pushout (in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$) of the identity morphism $\operatorname{id}_{D}$ with itself along $f$; in particular, if $w$ belongs to $\overline{W}$, then so does $\gamma _{w}$. It follows that $W'$ is contained in $\overline{W}$. Note that if $C$ and $D$ are $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact, then $D \coprod _{C} D$ is also $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact (Proposition <a data-tag="064W" href="/tag/064W">9.2.5.24</a>). Moreover, if the morphism spaces $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( C, E)$ and $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(D,E)$ are essentially $\lambda $-small for some object $E \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, then $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( D \coprod _{C} D, E)$ is also essentially $\lambda $-small (Variant <a data-tag="03UB" href="/tag/03UB">7.4.1.15</a>). Consequently, the collection of morphisms $W'$ also satisfies conditions $(1)$ and $(2)$, and therefore satisfies the hypotheses of Proposition <a data-tag="06VZ" href="/tag/06VZ">9.6.3.9</a>. 
    <p>
      Applying Proposition <a data-tag="06VZ" href="/tag/06VZ">9.6.3.9</a>, we conclude that for every object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ there is a morphism $f: X \rightarrow Y$, where $Y$ is weakly $W'$-local and $f$ is a $\lambda $-small transfinite pushout of morphisms belonging to $W'$, and therefore belongs to $\overline{W}$. We conclude by observing that the object $Y$ is automatically $W'$-local (Proposition <a data-tag="04LY" href="/tag/04LY">9.6.2.14</a>), and therefore $W$-local. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
